bbapprove help config — the config file and where each setting comes from.

A TOML file; bbapprove.config.template lists every key (type, repository, author_email, disallow, update_repo,
user_token_name, delete_source_branch) with comments. Nothing site- or person-specific is built in. --print-config shows the
effective settings and which of flag, env, file or default each came from (never a secret).

Lookup: the first that exists wins: --config FILE, BBAPPROVE_CONFIG (both must exist), <repo>/bbapprove.config.toml
(the install root: the parent of the binary's bin/ directory, or the binary's own directory when it is not in
bin/, as in the Windows archive; git-ignored), then $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bbapprove/config.toml
(default ~/.config/bbapprove/config.toml).

Precedence per setting: command line > environment > file > built-in default. Environment names:
BBAPPROVE_REPOSITORY, BBAPPROVE_AUTHOR_EMAIL, BBAPPROVE_TOKEN_NAME, BBAPPROVE_UPDATE_REPO. Only
type ("bitbucket"), update_repo and delete_source_branch have defaults; repository and author_email are required and bbapprove exits 1
naming the missing one and the paths searched. disallow is the union of the file, --disallow /
BBAPPROVE_DISALLOW (comma-separated) and the optional BBAPPROVE_DISALLOW_FILE (one dependency per line;
default disallow.txt in the repo root, git-ignored). type "bitbucket" runs the adapter built into the binary (no bash, curl or jq needed), any other type runs
adapters/<type>, and BBAPPROVE_ADAPTER (an executable path) overrides both.
delete_source_branch (default true) deletes the PR's source branch after a successful merge; set it to false
in the file or with BBAPPROVE_DELETE_SOURCE_BRANCH (true, false, 1, 0, yes, no; there is no command-line flag).
Unknown keys only warn; malformed TOML stops the run and names the file and line.

First run: with no config file, a run on a terminal offers to create $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bbapprove/config.toml
(mode 0600, never overwrites) from the template built into the binary, opens it in $VISUAL/$EDITOR, re-prompts
while the example values are unchanged (at most 3 rounds), then continues with your original command. In CI or
pipes nothing is written; the error shows the mkdir and cp commands to run.
--init does the same on request: it writes to --config FILE, else BBAPPROVE_CONFIG, else the default path, and
refuses to replace an existing file unless --force.
